\paragraph{Relaxing translational equivariance:}
\label{sec:relaxing-translation-equivariance}
In Figure~\ref{fig:relaxing-trans-eq}, we see that HaloNet-50 with $\BlockSize=8$, and $\HaloSize=3$ achieves better accuracies using the same block and halo to achieve $7\times7$ neighborhoods with attention masks ~\cite{ramachandran2019standalone} and the gap widens with more regularizations. This suggests that larger receptive fields are more important than inductive biases such as translational equivariance.

\paragraph{Window and halo size:} When using the blocked input format, there are two ways of changing the window size of attention: changing the query block size or the halo size. For the same window size $\WindowSize$, smaller query blocks and larger halos require more memory than larger query blocks and smaller halos, as discussed in section~\ref{sec:improved-impl}.

